{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"reasoning","text":"The data is a binned genome-wide count table: rows are genomic bins (chromosome, start position, end position) at 10 kb resolution (or variable), columns are organs: bladder, brain, cloaca, gallbladder, gill, heart, intestine, kidney, limb, liver, lung, pancreas, prostate, spleen, stomach. So there are 15 organs.\n\nTask: determine whether two organ labels have been swapped. The strategy is as follows:\n\n1. The count table has rows = genomic bins, columns = organs. If two organ labels are swapped, the columns themselves are intact, but their names are exchanged. Therefore, we need to identify which two organ columns have profiles that are inconsistent with their own labels.\n\n2. Approach: use marker genes / promoters. For each organ, there are known marker genes whose promoters should be accessible (ATAC peaks) in that organ. Using the GTF annotation, compute promoter accessibility (e.g., counts within ±2 kb of TSS) per gene per organ. Then, for each pair of organs, compute a \"swap score\": how much better the data matches when the labels of the two organs are exchanged.\n\n3. Another approach: compare against public source profiles (e.g., published axolotl ATAC-seq). But we can't rely on external data — well, the network is available, but it's better to rely on internal evidence.\n\nActually, the classical method for detecting sample swaps in ATAC-seq count tables is:\n\n- Compute the correlation (e.g., Pearson/Spearman) between all pairs of samples across bins. If two organs' labels are swapped, that alone wouldn't directly reveal it from pairwise correlations, because the columns are just renamed...\n\nWait, let me think carefully. If labels are swapped, the data columns themselves are intact. That is, the column named \"Brain\" actually contains \"Heart\" data, and vice versa. To detect this, we need to use prior biological knowledge: each organ should have specific marker genes with accessible promoters.\n\nTherefore, the approach:\n1. Load the GTF, extract genes with TSS coordinates.\n2. For each organ, compute promoter accessibility per gene: sum of counts in the bins overlapping the promoter region (TSS ± 2kb, or TSS ± 5kb).\n3. Define marker gene sets for each axolotl organ. Axolotl gene names in AmexG v6 — the GTF should have gene symbols. Known markers:\n   - Brain: syt1, snap25, gfap, mbp, tubb2b, nefl\n   - Heart: myh6, myh7, tnnt2, actc1, nppa, myl2\n   - Liver: alb, apoa1, hnf4a, cyp3a, tf (transferrin), apob\n   - Kidney: slc12a1 (TAL), aqp2 (collecting duct), slc12a3 (DCT), umod, pax2, slc22a6\n   - Lung: sftpa1, sftpb, sftpc, nkx2-1 (also thyroid), scgb1a1\n   - Pancreas: ins1/ins2, gcg, sst, pdx1, amylase (amy2a), cpa1, elastase (ela3a/ela3b), ptf1a\n   - Spleen: immune markers — cd3e, cd8a, cd19, ms4a1, lck, hbb (blood)\n   - Intestine: villin (vil1), cdx2, fabp2, muc2\n   - Stomach: pga3/pepsinogen, ghrl, atp4a, muc5ac, chga\n   - Limb: muscle/connective tissue markers? Limb could be muscle: acta1, myh; or connective tissue: col1a1, col2a1\n   - Gill: hemoglobin? Axolotl gills express globins (oxygen exchange), so hba/hbb. Gill is also involved in ion transport: atp1a1, slc12a2. Hmm.\n   - Bladder: upk (uroplakin), krt, upk1b, upk2, upk3a\n   - Prostate: klk, nkx3-1, msmb, acpp\n   - Cloaca: similar to intestine/bladder\n   - GallBladder: slc4a2? fabp? Actually, gallbladder markers: anxa4, slc4a2, fabp1? Hmm, the gallbladder epithelium shares markers with the biliary tract: krt19, sox9, opn3?\n\nThis is getting complex. The data probably contains two swapped organs. A robust approach without needing perfectly curated marker gene sets is:\n\nUse \"marker coherence\": for each organ pair (A, B), test the hypothesis that the labels are swapped. If organs A and B are swapped, then:\n- Organ A's marker genes should be more accessible in the column labeled \"B\".\n- Organ B's marker genes should be more accessible in the column labeled