{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"reasoning","text":"The GTF uses chromosome names like `chr1p`, `chr1q`, …, `chr14p`, `chr14q` — the chromosome arms. In the count table, these arms are split into contigs named `chr1p_1`, `chr1p_2`, etc.\n\nKey question: How do the contigs in the count table map to coordinates on the GTF arms? If the contigs are concatenated in order (`_1`, `_2`, `_3`…), then contig k's offset = sum of sizes of preceding contigs. Let me verify: max position of chr1p in the GTF = 1,417,632,714. Sum of sizes of chr1p_1 + chr1p_2 + chr1p_3 = 472,565,920 + 475,274,080 + 469,857,632 = 1,417,697,632. Close to 1,417,632,714 (max gene position) — consistent with concatenation.\n\nBut wait — is this ordering guaranteed? This is an assumption. Alternative possibility: the coordinates of the count table within each contig could be local (contig-local coordinates), and the GTF coordinates could be arm-global. If concatenation holds, then GTF position p within arm `chr1p` maps to contig `chr1p_k` with k such that offset_k <= p < offset_k + size_k.\n\nLet me verify by checking the contig names and sizes in the chrom.sizes file for all arms and comparing the totals with the GTF chromosome lengths. I also need to check whether the chrom.sizes file contains only arm contigs or also includes unplaced contigs (C000xxxx). Let me look at the whole chrom.sizes file (1133 bytes — small)."}}
