{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"reasoning","text":"chrom.sizes has 63 contigs: chromosome arms, some of which are split into `_1`, `_2`, `_3` parts. Therefore the mapping is: concatenate the split parts in numerical order to reconstruct the arm coordinates used in the GTF. Verify that the total size of the parts for each arm ≈ the GTF arm length (max position of a gene + a bit extra).\n\nLet me verify a few:\n- chr9p: single contig 455,256,413 vs GTF max 454,654,942 ✓\n- chr10q: single 525,957,290 vs GTF 525,316,404 ✓\n- chr11p: single 305,732,125 vs GTF 305,462,982 ✓\n- chr12p: single 289,110,169 vs GTF 288,944,090 ✓\n- chr13p: single 241,817,284 vs GTF 241,560,448 ✓\n- chr14p/q ✓\n- chr1p parts: 472,565,920+475,274,080+469,857,632 = 1,417,697,632 vs GTF max 1,417,632,714 ✓ (difference of about 65kb, no problem)\n\nOK, mapping: contig `chrXp_k` → arm `chrXp`, with offset = sum of sizes of `chrXp_1..k-1`. Unsplit arms (chr9p, chr10q, etc.) map directly with offset 0.\n\nNow, the plan:\n\n**Step 1: Build a promoter interval set from the GTF.**\n- Parse gene lines (feature == 'gene') to get chr, strand, start, end, gene_id, gene_name.\n- Compute TSS: for + strand, tss = start; for - strand, tss = end.\n- Promoter window: TSS ± 2000 bp (standard).\n- gene_name is in a format like \"ZFP37 [nr]|ZNF568 [hs]\" — extract the human symbol (the part with [hs]), and the nr symbol if possible. Build a searchable symbol list per gene.\n\n**Step 2: Extract promoter-overlapping bins from the count table.**\n- Iterate over TSV in chunks; for each row compute (arm, global position). For bins that overlap a promoter window, add counts.\n- To do this efficiently: precompute per-arm sorted lists of promoter intervals; for each bin row, binary-search to find overlapping promoters. Each bin is 10kb and promoters are ±2kb, so each bin overlaps at most a few promoters. Or, more simply: for each promoter, compute which bin indices it overlaps: bins are fixed 10kb windows (start position appears to be multiples of 10kb: 10000-20000, 20000-30000, 70000-80000 — looks like a 10kb tiled bin with some gaps where bins have zero counts? Actually probably the bin exists only when count > 0 in at least one sample, or the bins are tiled and rows with all zero counts are omitted). Let me check whether all bins are present: 2.43M rows × 10kb = 24.3 Gb covered out of a 32.1 Gb genome — so about 76% of bins are present (the rest probably have all-zero counts, removed by the q1 filter — the filename has \"q1\").\n\nSo bins are 10kb tiles; for each promoter window [tss-2000, tss+2000], overlapping bins = those with start in [floor((tss-2000)/10000)*10000, ...]. Then look up counts for those (chrom, start) bins via hash join.\n\nApproach in pandas:\n- Read entire TSV (2.43M rows × 18 cols) — with dtypes: chrom as category, start/end as int32, counts as int32. Memory ≈ 2.43M × (16×4 + ~12) ≈ ~190MB. No problem.\n- Build a promoter table with columns arm, bin_start (possibly multiple rows per promoter for each overlapping bin), gene_id, symbol.\n- Merge count table with promoter-bin table on (arm, start) — but the count table chrom is the contig name; first convert contig → (arm, offset) and global_start = offset + start.\n- Then merge. Sum counts per gene (over all promoter bins) — or average per bp. Promoter length is constant (±2kb, possibly clipped at contig boundaries), so the sum is fine.\n\n**Step 3: Marker genes.**\nDefine per-organ marker lists (using human symbols; axolotl GTF has human ortholog annotations). Need to be careful about which symbols exist. Let me extract all [hs] symbols from the GTF and check for presence of candidate markers.\n\nCandidate marker genes per organ (human ortholog symbols):\n- Brain: SYP, SNAP25, GFAP, MBP, PLP1, TUBB3, NEFL, NEFM, GAD1, SLC17A7, OLIG2, MOBP, MAG\n- Heart: MYH6, MYH7, TNNT2, MYL2, MYL3, ACTC1, NPPA, NPPB, RYR2, TNNI3, TPM1\n- Liver: ALB, APOA1, APOB, APOC3, TFR (TF), HNF4A, SERPINA1, CYP3A4, FGA, FGG, FGB, HP, VT, TF, AGT\n- Kidney: SLC12A1, UMOD, AQP2, SLC12A3, CALB1, SLC22A6, SLC22A8, LRP2, CUBN, NPHS1, NPHS2, PAX2, GATA3\n- Lung: SFTPA1, SFTPB, SFTPC, SFTPD, SCGB1A1, NKX2-1, AGER, PDPN, ABCA3\n- Pancreas: INS, GCG, SST, PPY, PDX1, AMY1 (AMY2A/AMY2B), CPA1, CPB1, CELA3A, CELA3B, CEL, PTF1A, GJA1? Plus digestive enzymes: trypsin PRSS1/2/3, chymotrypsin CTRB1/2, lipase PNLIP\n- Spleen: immune — CD3D/CD3E/CD3G, CD8A/B, CD4, CD19, MS4A1 (CD20), CD79A, LCK, IL7R, SPI1 (PU.1), LYZ, HBB/HBA (blood-rich organ), PTPRC (CD45)\n- Intestine: VIL1, CDX2, FABP2 (I-FABP), FABP6, MUC2, SI (sucrase-isomaltase), ALPI, ANPEP (APN), DPP4, RBP2?\n- Stomach: PGA3/PGA4/PGA5 (pepsinogen A), PGC (pepsinogen C), GHRL, ATP4A, ATP4B, CHGA, MUC5AC, MUC6, TFF2?\n- Limb: this is tricky. Axolotl limb = muscle + cartilage/bone + skin. Muscle markers: ACTA1, MYH1/MYH2/MYH4 (skeletal myosin), TNNT1/TNNI1/TNNI2, MYOG? Cartilage: COL2A1, ACAN, SOX9, COMP; Bone: COL1A1, SPP1, BGLAP; Skin: KRT5/KRT14. The limb might also include a lot of blood. Hmm.\n- Gill: axolotl gills are respiratory organs with lots of blood → globins HBA/HBB/HBM; ion transporters: SLC12A2 (NKCC1), ATP1A1, carbonic anhydrase CA2/CA4/CA12; also MUC5B? Filamentous structures: keratins. In amphibians, larval gills express hemoglobin strongly. CA2 too.\n- Bladder: uroplakins UPK1B, UPK2, UPK3A, UPK1A; KRT7/KRT20? Also SLC14A2? In axolotl, the bladder stores water/ions. Uroplakins are the best.\n- Prostate: NKX3-1, MSMB, KLK2/KLK3 (PSA), ACPP, STEAP4? Androgen-regulated genes. In axolotl... hmm, the prostate homolog in urodeles — does the axolotl have a prostate? Amphibians have a \"prostate\" (pars dissecta?). Anyway, markers: NKX3-1, MSMB, KLKs.\n- Cloaca: common chamber — markers for intestinal + urinary + squamous epithelium: VIL1 (hindgut part), UPK (urodeal part), KRTs. Cloacal glands? Hmm.\n- Gallbladder: biliary epithelium: KRT19, SOX9, ANXA4, SLC4A2 (AE2), MUC1? Also FABP1? Gallbladder shares a developmental origin with the liver (both from hepatic diverticulum). SLC4A2, ANXA4, KRT19 are used as biliary markers. Hmm.\n\nBecause marker assignment can be ambiguous, a robust approach is to combine several lines of evidence:\n\n**Evidence 1 — Organ marker coherence (directional test):** For each ordered organ pair, compute the accessibility of organ X's marker genes in each organ column; organ X's markers should have their highest (or near-highest) accessibility in column X. If X's markers are maximally accessible in column Y and Y's markers are maximally accessible in column X, that's a swap.\n\nDefine per-organ marker score: for organ X with marker set M_X, let a_m(c) = normalized promoter accessibility of marker gene m in column c. Within each marker gene, compute a z-score across organs, then average. Then:\n- self_consistency(X) = mean z of M_X markers in column X.\n- For pair (X,Y): swapped_consistency = mean z of M_X markers in column Y + mean z of M_Y markers in column X vs current = mean z of M_X in X + M_Y in Y.\n- swap_score(X,Y) = (z(M_X→Y) + z(M_Y→X)) − (z(M_X→X) + z(M_Y→Y)). Positive → supports the swap.\n\n**Evidence 2 — Genome-wide correlation structure:** If two organs' labels are swapped, the correlation structure itself doesn't reveal it (columns are just relabeled). However, if there's an expected similarity tree (e.g., brain and spinal cord are similar; digestive organs cluster together), we can check consistency with known organ biology: e.g., Stomach and Intestine should be correlated (both digestive tract); Liver and Pancreas moderately; etc. But this is weak.\n\nAlternative: **Public source profiles**. The task says we may compare against public source profiles. This dataset might originate from a published axolotl ATAC-seq atlas. Hmm. Maybe we can search GEO for axolotl bulk ATAC-seq across organs. Network is enabled. But the risk of getting the wrong dataset is high, and this is probably a synthetic benchmark. Let me first look at the internal marker evidence, then decide whether external comparison is needed.\n\n**Evidence 3 — Library size is a trap:** The notes warn against using library size alone. So some organ columns may have anomalously large/small totals. Let me compute totals.\n\nAdditional consideration: a swap could occur between two very similar organs (e.g., Stomach and Intestine, or Bladder and Cloaca), in which case detection would be difficult. The task says \"if the evidence doesn't support a unique swap, return swap_detected=false and explain the uncertainty.\"\n\nOK, let me write the analysis script step by step. First, exploration:\n\n1. Total counts per organ.\n2. Check what symbols are in the GTF; grep candidate markers.\n3. Build the promoter matrix.\n\nLet me first compute totals per organ and some simple QC.\n"}}
