C16, GKN1/2?; GHRL missing; check GAST?; CHGB?; also SLC9A2?; stomach too: Pepsinogen is the key.\n- Intestine: VIL1, CDX2, FABP2, ALPI, ANPEP, DPP4, SI, MUC2 present; check FABP6, RBP2?; ISX?; LCT?; SLC15A1?\n- Bladder: UPK1B, UPK3A present; check UPK1A, UPK2 (missing), KRT20 missing...; also SLC14A2?; check KRT13?; urothelium also has FOXA2?; and check placental? No.\n- Cloaca: hard to define markers; cloaca is a mixture of hindgut + urothelium + squamous. Use a mixed expectation: VIL1/CDX2 low-to-medium, UPK medium, KRT present. Hmm. Maybe the swap involves the cloaca and something else.\n- Gallbladder: KRT19, SOX9, ANXA4, SLC4A2 present; check MUC1, EPCAM, SPP1, AQP1?; biliary: also check KRT7 (missing), KRT8/KRT18.\n\nLet's check the presence/absence of these additional symbols. Also important: multiple genes may share symbols (e.g., paralogs — the axolotl is allotetraploid... actually the axolotl is diploid but with many duplications). Take all genes matching a symbol.\n\nNext, marker sets. Let's be careful and conservative: use genes with strong, well-established organ specificity, and require multiple markers per organ.\n\nAnother consideration: the swap may involve organs with few distinctive markers (Cloaca, GallBladder, Prostate, Limb). The design is likely to have swapped a pair where marker evidence is decisive.\n\nSo, let's check the additional symbols.\n"}}
