{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting! The top pair is GallBladder ↔ Stomach, score 2.18, and this is the only pair where both directions are strongly positive (+1.36, +0.82). Other pairs involving Stomach are driven almost exclusively by a single direction (Stomach markers being better elsewhere), but the reverse direction is ~0 or negative.\n\nBut wait — earlier when I looked at individual markers, GB markers had their argmax in Prostate (SLC4A2 3.00) and Cloaca (SOX9 1.71, KRT8 1.43, KRT18 1.31, MUC1 1.80). So why is the mean z of GB markers in the Stomach column +0.82? Let me look at the GB set in the Stomach column: KRT19 0.10, KRT8 0.47, KRT18 1.16, SOX9 1.07, ANXA4 1.45, SLC4A2 -0.35, MUC1 0.99, EPCAM 0.88, FOXA1 0.69. Averaging these: (0.10+0.47+1.16+1.07+1.45-0.35+0.99+0.88+0.69)/9 = 6.46/9 = 0.72. With paralogs included it becomes 0.82. OK.\n\nAnd Stomach markers in the GB column: PGA3 0.37, PGC nan, CHGA -0.08, CHGB -1.64, MUC5AC nan, TFF2 -1.01, GKN1 0.31, GKN2 1.57, ATP4B 0.43, MUC1 -0.35 → mean of non-nan ≈ (0.37-0.08-1.64-1.01+0.31+1.57+0.43-0.35)/8 = -0.40/8 = -0.05?? But the table says Stomach→GallBladder: +0.82. Hmm, big discrepancy — again the paralog expansion must be changing things a lot. I need to look at the actual expanded gene lists and their z's.\n\nHmm, this discrepancy bothers me. Stomach→GallBladder delta = MZ[Stomach][GB] - MZ[Stomach][Stomach] = 0.82 improvement... MZ[Stomach][Stomach] = -0.74, so MZ[Stomach][GB] = +0.08? The earlier matrix showed Stomach marker set in GallBladder column = 0.09. So delta = 0.09 - (-0.74) = 0.83. I see, so Stomach markers aren't high in the GB column (0.09), they're just much higher than in the Stomach column (-0.74). The GB↔Stomach pair score is being driven by: GB markers are decent in the Stomach column (+1.36 over self), and Stomach markers are 0.83 higher in the GB column than in the Stomach column — even though the absolute value is only 0.09.\n\nThis is the problem with delta-based scores when self-consistency is very negative: swapping two wrong labels can look \"good\" even if neither column matches the other organ's profile. A true swap should show absolute cross-consistency: A's markers are high in column B AND B's markers are high in column A.\n\nLet me define a stricter score: swap_support(A,B) = min(MZ[A][B], MZ[B][A]) or the average of absolute cross z's. For a true swap, both cross values should be high in absolute terms.\n\nCompute cross matrix:\n- GB markers in Stomach column: 0.09 (not high!)\n- Stomach markers in GB column: 0.09?? Wait, the matrix has row=Stomach, col=GallBladder = 0.09. And row=GallBladder, col=Stomach = 0.66.\n\nSo absolute cross values: GB→Stomach column = 0.66, Stomach→GB column = 0.09. In absolute terms, not strong either way.\n\nCompare with GallBladder↔Prostate: GB markers in Prostate column = 0.95 (high!), Prostate markers in GB column = -0.57 (low!). One-sided only.\n\nGallBladder↔Cloaca: GB markers in Cloaca column = 0.89 (high!), Cloaca markers in GB column = -0.15. One-sided.\n\nHmm. So the GB column doesn't match any other single column's profile well. The GB markers want to be in {Prostate 0.95, Cloaca 0.89, Gill 0.79, Stomach 0.66}... spread out.\n\nHmm, hmm, hmm. So what's really going on? Possibilities:\n\n(a) The GallBladder column contains a mixture or damaged tissue (low-quality sample), and its true identity signal is weak.\n\n(b) Marker set problem: my GB markers (biliary program: KRT8/18/19, SOX9, ANXA4, SLC4A2, MUC1, EPCAM, FOXA1) might partially mark the gallbladder but also other things. SOX9 (neural crest, chondrocytes, pancreas, testis...), EPCAM (all epithelia), MUC1 (many epithelia), FOXA1 (liver/gut/prostate/lung). ANXA4 and KRT19 are the most biliary-specific. SLC4A2 is ubiquitous (erythrocyte AE2!). Hmm, SLC4A2 is a erythrocyte anion exchanger — should be high in blood-rich samples! Its argmax is Prostate 3.00?? Strange.\n\n(c) The actual swap mi