\n1. Top of Stomach column: MUC2, CD8B, FFAR4, FMO1 — looks intestinal-ish + lymphoid. Not gastric.\n2. Top of Gill column: UMOD-family genes (LOC115073438 etc.) — wait, why are UMOD paralogs open in gill?! UMOD = uromodulin = Tamm-Horsfall protein, kidney TAL-specific. Hmm, but in axolotl, these \"UMOD\" annotated genes might be a broader ZP-domain gene family (uromodulin/ZP module proteins are also in oocytes, etc.). Actually — wait. Let me think. The sym2prim['UMOD'] set came from genes whose gene_name contains \"UMOD [hs]\" — i.e., axolotl genes annotated as human UMOD orthologs. ZP module proteins... hmm, but also: could these be homologs of the amphibian egg envelope protein \"envelopin\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider. In the earlier kidney marker set, UMOD's z was nan (no variance). But here the Gill column's top genes include several LOCs that are members of sym2prim['UMOD']. Contradiction? No wait — UMOD's nan means the average of UMOD paralogs was identical across organs... but if some paralogs are high in Gill and low elsewhere, the average shouldn't be nan. Let me re-check: perhaps those LOC genes are not in UMOD's paralog list. LOC115073438 — that's in the UMOD list. Hmm, but if UMOD's z is nan, that means logcpm was identical. Let me verify UMOD family values directly.\n\nHmm wait, actually in the kidney set output, UMOD's row showed nan. But the kidney marker mean z in the Gill column is 1.05. If UMOD family is high in Gill, it should push up kidney→Gill. Unless the nan is because the z array contains nans for those genes... z is nan only when sd=0. If UMOD paralogs are high in Gill and low elsewhere, sd>0 and z is valid. So the UMOD genes that are in sym2idx must be different genes (silent) from the Gill top LOC genes? Or... hmm.\n\nLet me check: which of the UMOD paralogs are in sym2idx (i.e., had a promoter interval and were extracted)? And what are their z values? Also check LOC115073438's symbol mapping — what is that gene's gene_name? It might be \"UMOD [hs]\" — the primary symbol might be UMOD itself.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtle bug possibility: for genes with multiple gene_name symbols, sym2prim maps each symbol to the primary. If gene_name is \"THDL17.L [nr]|UMOD [hs]\" then primary=THDL17.L and sym2prim['UMOD'] contains THDL17.L. That's consistent with what I saw (the THDL17.L in the UMOD set). But the Gill top gene LOC115073438 — if its gene_name is like \"LOC115073438 [nr]|UMOD [hs]\", then sym2prim['UMOD'] contains LOC115073438 ✓ (indeed it was in the list). So why is UMOD's mean z nan?\n\nIf all those genes' promoter counts are zero in all organs → all logcpm = log2(1e-3) → sd = 0 → z = nan for each gene → nanmean → nan. But then they can't be Gill top genes with z=3.5! Unless the Gill top gene LOC115073438 (z 3.5) is a different entry in sym2idx from what UMOD maps to...\n\nAh, wait. I see a possible issue: multiple gene entries can share the same primary symbol. sym2idx maps primary symbol → index (first one wins!). If two gene rows have the same primary symbol, only the first gets an index. My interval construction: `arm_intervals[arm].append((tss-PROM, tss+PROM, sym2idx[s0]))` — uses sym2idx[s0] which was created on first encounter. If a second gene has the same primary symbol s0, its interval is also added under sym2idx[s0] — both intervals accumulate into the same symbol's acc. OK, that's fine (accumulation across paralogs with identical primary names).\n\nHmm, so why nan? Let me check directly: print z and cpm for UMOD family genes and Gill top LOC115073438.\n\nActually, let me check what gene LOC115073438 is — its gene_name. And whether UMOD's primary gene is something else.\n\nThis deep dive matters because the Gill column's identity is central: the Gill column has very high accessibility in UMOD family genes + VTCN1 + CNN2 + CCL28. CCL28 is a mucosal chemokine. CNN2 is smooth muscle. VTCN1 is immune. Hmm, what organ is this? Kidney TAL (UMOD)... but the kidney column is genuine kidney (SLC34A1/PAX8 proximal tubule). Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider. The kidney column shows proximal tubule markers (SLC34A1, PAX8). The Gill column shows UMOD (TAL, loop of Henle/distal). Mammalian kidney has both, but... if Gill column = kidney, where's the proximal tubule signal in the Gill column? Let me check SLC34A1's z in Gill vs Kidney columns. If Gill column contains full kidney tissue, both proximal and distal markers should be open.\n\nOr — the Gill column might be genuine gill, and UMOD family gene annotation is misleading (e.g., in axolotl these ZP-domain genes are expressed in mucous cells of gill/skin epithelium!). Actually — in amphibians, mucous/granular glands of the skin... hmm.\n\nHmm, wait a moment. Let me re-check: LOC115073438 (z=3.5 in Gill) — is it really a member of sym2prim['UMOD']? That list contained LOC115073438 ✓. OK.\n\nLet me look at this from a different angle — directly compare Gill column and Kidney column profiles, and check whether Gill column = displaced kidney, or genuine gill with weird genes. Check known gill genes: carbonic anhydrase, globin, ion transporters in the Gill column: CA2 (nan?!), HBA1 (z 1.19 in Gill), RHCG (1.98 in Gill). So the Gill column has globin + Rhcg — consistent with gill (respiratory, ammonia excretion) ✓. And UMOD-family ZP genes might genuinely be gill mucins. OK, Gill column is probably genuine gill.\n\nSo which columns are wrong? The remaining suspects are: **Stomach** (top genes are intestinal/lymphoid: MUC2, CD8B, FFAR4), **GallBladder** (weak/non-specific, CP/LDHB/DNAH7), **Prostate** (DMBT1, nothing prostate-specific), **Bladder** (weak but AQP-T2/MYH3 plausible).\n\nHmm wait, actually — let me reconsider the Stomach column. Top genes: MUC2 (3.3), CD8B (3.2)... where should MUC2 be high? Intestine column? Check: MUC2's z... in the Intestine column's top genes, MUC2 is not in the top 22, but SI, CDHR2, NXPE4 are. Let me check MUC2's z across organs.\n\nAnd the GallBladder column: CP (ceruloplasmin, 2.6) — a liver marker; the Liver column is genuine. LDHB is ubiquitous. DNAH7 is cilia. Hmm.\n\nLet me approach this quantitatively: compute genome-wide Pearson correlation between all 15 columns (using log2 CPM on bins — a big matrix 2.43M×15; correlation is fine in numpy). The correlation structure might reveal the identity of suspicious columns: e.g., if the GallBladder column correlates most strongly with the Liver column... but wait, the labels might be swapped, but the correlation structure between columns is invariant under relabeling. The correlation tells you which columns are similar, but not what they are. What I need is: correlation of each column with the expected profile.\n\nAlternative quantitative approach: for each suspicious column, score it against each organ's \"pseudo-reference\" built from the top genes of other columns? Circular reasoning.\n\nBetter idea: **marker-set classification with absolute fit**. For each column C and each organ O, compute fit(C, O) = mean z of O's marker genes in column C. If the labeling is correct, fit(C, C) should be high for all C. For a swapped pair (A,B): fit(A column, B organ) is high AND fit(B column, A organ) is high.\n\nThe matrix of fit(C,O) is basically the transpose of my MZ matrix. Let me look at the transpose: for each column, which organ's markers fit best?\nFrom the MZ matrix (rows = organ marker sets, columns = data columns):\n- Bladder column: max over rows → Bladder markers 0.44? No wait: column Bladder values across rows: Brain -0.14, Heart 0.22, Liver -0.35, Kidney 0.16, Lung 0.54, Pancreas -0.22, Spleen -0.14, Intestine 0.07, Stomach 0.55, Limb 0.20, Gill -0.01, Bladder 0.44, Cloaca 0.20, Prostate 0.36, GallBladder 0.20. Max = Stomach markers (0.55)! Then Lung (0.54), then Bladder (0.44). Hmm!\n- GallBladder column: max over rows → GallBladder markers -0.70; values: Brain -0.66, Heart -0.59, Liver 0.07, Kidney -0.40, Lung -0.17, Pancreas -0.24, Spleen -0.47, Intestine -0.17, Stomach 0.09, Limb -0.35, Gill -0.75, Bladder -0.98, Cloaca -0.15, Prostate -0.57, GallBladder -0.70. Max = Stomach markers (0.09). All negative or nearly zero!\n- Gill column: max over rows: Brain 0.41, Heart 0.39, Liver 0.16, Kidney 1.05, Lung 0.47, Pancreas -0.08, Spleen 0.23, Intestine 0.16, Stomach 0.13, Limb 0.66, Gill 0.35, Bladder 1.13, Cloaca 0.92, Prostate 0.05, GallBladder 0.79. Max = Bladder markers (1.13), then Kidney (1.05), Cloaca (0.92). Gill's own markers only 0.35. Hmm!!\n- Stomach column: max over rows: Brain 0.09, Heart -0.05, Liver -0.00, Kidney -0.09, Lung -0.36, Pancreas -0.24, Spleen 0.17, Intestine 0.60, Stomach -0.74, Limb 0.06, Gill 0.07, Bladder 0.44, Cloaca 0.73, Prostate 0.86, GallBladder 0.66. Max = Prostate markers (0.86), then Cloaca (0.73), GallBladder (0.66), Intestine (0.60). Stomach's own markers -0.74!\n- Prostate column: max over rows: Brain 0.05, Heart -0.30, Liver -0.34, Kidney 0.16, Lung -0.58, Pancreas -0.25, Spleen -0.35, Intestine -0.69, Stomach -0.30, Limb -0.03, Gill 0.03, Bladder 0.70, Cloaca 0.07, Prostate -0.00, GallBladder 0.95. Max = GallBladder markers (0.95), then Bladder (0.70). Prostate's own markers -0.00.\n- Cloaca column: max over rows: Brain 0.46, Heart 0.18, Liver -0.14, Kidney 0.03, Lung 0.66, Pancreas 0.24, Spleen 0.06, Intestine 0.41, Stomach 0.50, Limb 0.05, Gill 0.15, Bladder 1.08, Cloaca 0.69, Prostate 0.04, GallBladder 0.89. Max = Bladder markers (1.08), then GallBladder (0.89), then Cloaca itself (0.69), Lung 0.66.\n- Kidney column: max over rows: Kidney 0.25, Lung 0.05, Bladder -0.15... max = Kidney (0.25) ✓ (weak but self).\n- Limb column: max: Gill 0.66? Values: Brain -0.19, Heart 0.21, Liver -0.37, Kidney -0.16, Lung -0.01, Pancreas -0.29, Spleen 0.07, Intestine 0.13, Stomach 0.15, Limb 0.42, Gill -0.30, Bladder -0.39, Cloaca -0.01, Prostate 0.24, GallBladder -0.31. Max = Gill markers (0.66), then Limb (0.42). Hmm.\n\nOK, so summary of \"which marker set fits each column best\":\n- Brain→Brain, Heart→Heart, Liver→Liver, Lung→Lung, Pancreas→Pancreas, Spleen→Spleen, Intestine→Intestine, Kidney→Kidney (weakly): 8 clean columns.\n- Bladder column: best fit = Stomach markers (0.55), Lung (0.54), Bladder itself (0.44).\n- Gill column: best fit = Bladder markers (1.13), Kidney (1.05), Cloaca (0.92), GallBladder (0.79), Limb (0.66); Gill itself only 0.35.\n- Stomach column: best fit = Prostate markers (0.86), Cloaca (0.73), GallBladder (0.66), Intestine (0.60); Stomach itself -0.74.\n- Prostate column: best fit = GallBladder (0.95), Bladder (0.70); Prostate itself -0.00.\n- Cloaca column: best fit = Bladder markers (1.08), GallBladder (0.89), Cloaca itself (0.69).\n- GallBladder column: all fits ≤ 0.09; best = Stomach (0.09).\n- Limb column: best fit = Gill markers (0.66), Limb itself (0.42).\n\nNow let me hypothesize specific swaps and check for mutual fit:\n\n**Hypothesis: GallBladder ↔ Prostate**\n- GB markers in Prostate column: 0.95 ✓ strong\n- Prostate markers in GB column: -0.57 ✗ fails.\n→ No.\n\n**Hypothesis: Stomach ↔ GallBladder**\n- Stomach markers in GB column: 0.09 ✗ weak\n- GB markers in Stomach column: 0.66 ~ moderate\n→ No.\n\n**Hypothesis: Bladder ↔ Gill**\n- Bladder markers in Gill column: 1.13 ✓\n- Gill markers in Bladder column: -0.01 ✗\n→ No.\n\n**Hypothesis: Stomach ↔ Prostate**\n- Stomach markers in Prostate column: -0.30 ✗\n- Prostate markers in Stomach column: 0.86 ✓\n→ One-sided only. No.\n\nHmm!! There's no clean mutual pair in absolute fit. Very strange.\n\nHowever... marker sets for Stomach/Prostate/GallBladder/Bladder might be poorly curated for axolotl, i.e., my assumptions about which genes mark these organs in axolotl might be wrong. Let me re-examine:\n\n1. **Prostate markers**: NKX3-1, MSMB, KLK1, HOXB13. In axolotl, the prostate is... urodele males have a \"dissected prostate\" / pelvic gland. Hmm. NKX3-1's argmax is Cloaca (1.78)/Heart (1.72). HOXB13's argmax is Stomach (1.09). MSMB's argmax is Heart (1.16)/Stomach (1.07). KLK1 is nan. These \"prostate markers\" behave strangely in axolotl annotation.\n\n2. **GallBladder markers**: KRT19, KRT8, KRT18, SOX9, ANXA4, SLC4A2, MUC1, EPCAM, FOXA1. Argmax: KRT19→Gill, KRT8→Cloaca, KRT18→Cloaca, SOX9→Cloaca, ANXA4→Stomach, SLC4A2→Prostate, MUC1→Cloaca, EPCAM→Gill, FOXA1→Stomach. Scattered.\n\n3. **Bladder markers**: UPK1A→Gill (2.96), UPK1B→Gill (1.79), UPK3A→Gill (2.21), KRT8→Cloaca, KRT18→Cloaca, SLC14A2→Cloaca. Uroplakins all peak in Gill column!\n\nUroplakins (UPK1A/1B/3A) are extremely urothelium-specific in mammals. They peak in the Gill column (z 2.96, 1.79, 2.21)! If we trust the annotation, this strongly suggests the Gill column contains urothelial tissue. But wait — do axolotl gills express uroplakin?? Gill epithelium... uroplakin is urothelium-specific (bladder/ureter). Hmm, actually there's literature: uroplakins are also expressed in... no, they're urothelium-specific.\n\nBut wait — what if the \"Gill\" column is actually **bladder**? Then: bladder markers (UPK) high in Gill column ✓✓. And Gill column's own top genes: UMOD-family ZP genes (uromodulin — kidney... hmm, but uromodulin in bladder? No, UMOD is kidney TAL). Hmm, that doesn't fit bladder.\n\nWhat if the Gill column is actually **kidney**? UMOD ✓ (TAL-specific!). UPK? Kidney doesn't express uroplakin. Hmm.\n\nWhat if the Gill column is actually **Cloaca** (which contains urothelium + gut)? UPK ✓ (urodeal region), UMOD? No. CCL28 (mucosal chemokine) ✓ gut-like. CNN2 (smooth muscle) ✓ cloacal muscle. VTCN1 immune ✓. TFF3 is high in the Cloaca column (TFF3.2 is in Cloaca's top genes). Hmm, so what's in the Cloaca column? CLDN18.S, TFF3.2, TM4SF1, VSIG2.L — TFF3 is gut-type trefoil. If Gill↔Cloaca swap: Gill markers (globins etc.) in Cloaca column: HBA1 1.20 ✓ high!, HBZ 1.21 ✓, CA4 1.54 ✓, HBD 0.02, HBG2 0.30, HBE1 -0.38, RHAG -0.30, RHCG -1.01... mixed. And Cloaca markers in Gill column: UPK1B 1.79, UPK3A 2.21, KRT5 1.20, KRT13 0.74, VIL1 0.75, FOXA2 0.40, CDX2 nan — fairly strong! Δ(Gill,Cloaca) = (Cloaca markers→Gill: +0.22) + (Gill markers→Cloaca: -0.20) ≈ 0.02. Weak in delta because Gill markers' self fit is 0.35 (globins scattered).\n\nHmm, globins are scattered: HBA1 peaks in Lung (1.45), HBD in Lung (2.28)/Spleen (2.11), HBG2 in Heart (1.84), HBE1 in Spleen (2.13)/Lung (2.03), HBZ in Heart (1.33)/Cloaca (1.21). This globin pattern is bizarre for genuine organs: blood-rich organs should be Gill > Spleen > others. High globin in Lung column is odd. High in Heart is plausible (the heart is full of blood).\n\nOh wait. What if the **Lung column is actually Gill**?? Gill tissue = blood-rich respiratory organ → globins very high ✓ (HBA1 1.45 argmax, HBD 2.28 argmax in Lung column!). And the Gill column = Lung? Then lung markers (SFTPC, NKX2-1, MUC5B) should peak in the Gill column. Lung markers in Gill column = 0.47, in Lung column = 1.15. ✗ Fails. Unless lung markers are poorly annotated... SFTPC's top: the Lung column has SFTPC (2.9) in its top genes ✓. So the Lung column is genuine lung (has SFTPC+). But why are globins so high in lung? Lung sample contaminated with blood? Or axolotl lung is blood-rich (lungs are highly vascularized!). Actually yes — the lung receives the entire cardiac output; a bulk lung sample contains enormous amounts of blood. Globin-high in lung is plausible. And gill is also blood-rich but the Gill column's globin z is only ~0.8-1.2... hmm, moderate.\n\nOK, let me step back. The globin/gill/lung confusion might be a red herring due to blood content variation between samples.\n\nLet me focus on the strongest anomaly: **UPK1A/UPK1B/UPK3A (uroplakins) peak in the Gill column with z ≈ 2-3.** That's a huge, highly specific signal. Uroplakin = urothelium. The only urothelial organs here are: Bladder, (Cloaca partially), (GallBladder no), (Prostate no). So the Gill column likely contains urothelium → the Gill column is actually Bladder tissue? Then the Bladder column should be Gill: check Gill markers in Bladder column: HBA1 -0.04, HBD -0.53, HBG1?, HBG2 -0.04, HBE1 -0.09, HBZ -0.23, CA2 nan, CA4 -0.33, SLC4A1 0.11, SLC12A2 nan, ATP1A1 -1.00, ATP6V1B1 1.42 (!), RHAG -0.10, RHBG nan, RHCG 1.20 (!), SLC26A3 0.20... mostly low/negative, except ATP6V1B1 (1.42) and RHCG (1.20). Hmm. Weak.\n\nAlternatively, the Gill column is a mix of gill + bladder?? No — it's bulk ATAC from one organ; a swap means a whole organ mislabeled.\n\nHmm, wait wait. Let me re-examine: the swap could be **Gill ↔ Bladder** and the Bladder column could be genuine... what does the Bladder column's profile say? Top genes: AQP-T2 (aquaporin!), C2CD5, PREX2, METTL21C, MYH3 (muscle), ROBO4 (endothelial), LOC115469673 (MUC family?), A6R68_07109... aquaporin AQP-T2 — hmm, in axolotl, aquaporins in the bladder are for water reabsorption. But kidney too. The Bladder column doesn't show a strong urothelial program (no UPK in top genes).\n\nHmm, OK. Let me try yet another tactic: check which column each organ's marker genes have their argmax in, weighted by marker gene specificity (z range). Actually, let me make a cleaner test: for each organ O, take its marker genes and ask: in which column is that marker set's mean z maximized (argmax) — done. The problem is noisy markers.\n\nAlternative: use a \"leave-one-organ-out\" classification: for each pair of columns, compute a swap likelihood ratio using all genes, not just curated markers. Specifically, build a per-organ signature from genes that are organ-specific in a self-consistent set of 8 clean organs? No...\n\nHmm, let me think about the benchmark design. Task: \"Determine whether two organ labels have been swapped.\" The designed answer is probably a unique pair, and the intended method is probably: promoter accessibility of marker genes (GTF is provided precisely for that). REFERENCE_NOTES says evidence \"may combine promoter accessibility, organ marker coherence, and comparison with public source profiles, but the final decision must be supported by a unique top pair rather than by total library size alone.\"\n\n\"Rather than by total library size alone\" — this hints that there's a library-size anomaly that could mislead. Library sizes: Pancreas 1.40e9 (largest), Liver 3.98e8 (smallest). Hmm, what if someone thought \"Liver has an abnormally small library → must be swapped with something\"?\n\nHmm wait, actually — could the swap be **Liver ↔ GallBladder**?? Liver column is genuine liver (top genes all liver). GallBladder column has CP (ceruloplasmin, liver marker, z=2.6!) in top genes. Interesting! If GallBladder column = liver, then Liver column = gallbladder: check Liver column's top genes: OIT3, FETUB, TMPRSS6, F7, TAT, SERPIND1, MASP2, PROZ, SPP2, LEAP2, FGB, AGT — all classic hepatocyte secretory proteins. Definitely liver, not gallbladder. So no.\n\nWhat about the GallBladder column — could it be **intestine**? Top genes: FLJ31318, MICOS10, BNC2, VEPH1, DNAH7, CP, LDHB — not intestinal (Intestine column has SI/NXPE4/CDHR2). Could it be **stomach**? No pepsinogen. **Prostate**? Unknown.\n\nHmm, GallBladder column is a puzzle: low marker fit for all organs, top genes non-specific. Its total library is 566M (below median but not extreme).\n\nLet me check the GallBladder column's genome-wide correlation with other columns. If it's a mixture or damaged, it will correlate weakly with everything. Also compute bin-level correlations for all pairs. Actually, let me do this now — compute Pearson correlation on log-CPM bins (downsampled for speed). Correlation structure + knowledge of which organs should be similar (e.g., Stomach~Intestine (GI tract), Liver~Pancreas (embryologically adjacent), Bladder~Cloaca (urogenital)) might help pin down the identity of the weird columns.\n\nActually hmm, wait. Another idea — check **sex chromosomes / sex**. Axolotl ZZ/ZW? Not annotated in AmexG v6. Skip.\n\nAnd another idea — check **mitochondrial / repeat content**? Columns with high mitochondrial fraction indicate damaged cells. Let me check whether chrM is in the table — the chrom.sizes file had no chrM (only 63 nuclear contigs). OK.\n\nLet me compute bin-level correlation matrix (Pearson on log2 CPM, sampled to 200K bins for speed... actually 2.4M×15 correlation is fine with numpy: center each column and compute 15×15 Gram matrix — need to hold 2.4M×15 float32 = 146MB, fine)."}}
