{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_63","type":"reasoning","text":"Correlation structure. Interesting points: all correlations are high (0.6–0.93) except Heart (0.2–0.32, consistent with heart being a mesodermal outlier). ATAC across tissues is dominated by open chromatin common to housekeeping genes, so high background correlations are expected.\n\nNotable high correlations:\n- Gill–Lung: 0.928 (the highest off-diagonal value!)\n- Bladder–Limb: 0.932 (the highest!)\n- Bladder–Kidney: 0.917\n- Kidney–Limb: 0.922\n- Brain–Limb: 0.913\n- Cloaca–Intestine: 0.891, Cloaca–Liver: 0.831, Cloaca–Stomach: 0.867, Intestine–Stomach: 0.893, Liver–Intestine: 0.876, Liver–Pancreas: 0.891, Pancreas–Cloaca: 0.869, Pancreas–Intestine: 0.906.\n- GallBladder is relatively low against everything (max 0.877 with Pancreas, 0.806 with Liver, 0.721 with Lung, 0.710 with Gill, 0.706 with Bladder, 0.627–0.635 against Spleen/Prostate). GallBladder is overall the least correlated, most distinctive column.\n- Prostate–Bladder 0.880, Prostate–Spleen 0.856, Prostate–Kidney 0.868, Prostate–Limb 0.878.\n- Spleen–Limb 0.880, Spleen–Kidney 0.848.\n\nHmm, Gill–Lung 0.928 being the highest: gill and lung are both respiratory organs — biologically reasonable! Bladder–Kidney 0.917 is also biologically reasonable (urinary system). Bladder–Limb 0.932?? That's odd. Kidney–Limb 0.922?? Limb is oddly highly correlated with Bladder/Kidney/Brain/Spleen… the Limb column's library is large (1.15e9). Hmm.\n\nSince all correlations are high, these subtle differences are hard to interpret. The correlation structure is roughly consistent with the labels (Gill–Lung are respiratory organs, Bladder–Kidney are urinary organs, Cloaca–Intestine–Stomach–Liver are digestive organs). So there's no obvious smoking gun in the correlations…\n\nActually wait — one more thing to check: if Gill↔Lung were swapped, the correlation matrix would be invariant (relabeling rows/columns). Right — correlations are invariant under label permutations. Therefore correlations can never reveal a swap. Only marker-based identity can. OK.\n\nNow, let's go back to the marker matrix and think very carefully. The designed swap is probably between two of the organs whose marker sets I might be getting wrong. Let me reconsider which organs have the weakest self-consistency:\n\nSelf z: Stomach -0.74, GallBladder -0.70, Prostate -0.00, Kidney 0.25, Gill 0.35, Limb 0.42, Bladder 0.44, Cloaca 0.69, Heart 0.68, Intestine 0.91, Spleen 1.04, Lung 1.15, Brain 1.19, Liver 1.79, Pancreas 1.95.\n\nHmm, wait — actually I might be making an error: my z is computed per gene across organs with sd at ddof=0; genes with very small variance across organs get huge z values from tiny differences. That noise inflates random signals. Better approach: shrink the denominator (add a floor to sd), or rank-based. The scatter in the \"argmax\" genes (e.g., SLC4A2's argmax of 3.00 in Prostate) may be noise from low-variance genes.\n\nLet me redo this with a variance floor: z' = (x - mean)/max(sd, s0), where s0 = median sd across genes. This will down-weight genes with near-constant expression (which also includes nan genes). Then recompute marker coherence and pairwise scores.\n\nAlso, I should restrict markers to those that show organ-specific variance: only include genes with sd > floor (i.e., genes that are truly differentially accessible).\n\nLet me also reconsider marker sets for the confusing organs — maybe add better markers:\n- Stomach: PGA3 is the key one (pepsinogen A3); also, are there other pepsinogen genes? Check 'PGA', 'PGC' family members in the GTF: search for gene names containing 'pepsinogen' or PGA/PGC/PGBC symbols. Let me grep the GTF.\n- Bladder: uroplakins UPK1A/1B/3A (UPK2 missing). Also 'SLC14A2'? and check uroplakin-like ZP genes.\n- GallBladder: check ANXA4, SLC4A2, KRT19, SOX9 — also 'SPP1', 'TFF2'? Hmm.\n- Prostate: KLK family, MSMB, NKX3-1, HOXB13 — check which KLKs exist: KLK1 exists. Check 'KLKB1'? 'KLK'? Let me grep all KLKs in the GTF.\n\nAlso grep pepsinogens.\n"}}
