{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting: UPK2.S exists (with the \".S\" suffix — probably a short isoform designation). Also, there are the axolotl-specific globins HB-AM and HB-B (axolotl adult globins!). CA1/CA3 etc. also exist.\n\nLet's check these additional genes:\n- UPK2.S — uroplakin 2! Add to the bladder marker.\n- HB-AM, HB-B — adult globins; important for blood-rich organs.\n- KLK7, KLKB1 also exist.\n\nAlso, let's check the \".S\" and \".L\" suffixes — these seem to indicate splice variants annotated as separate genes (S = short, L = long).\n\nNow, a key improvement: rebuild z using a variance floor. Then refine the marker sets:\n\n- Bladder: UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK2.S, UPK3A, UPK3B, KRT8, KRT18, SLC14A2.\n- Gill: add HB-AM, HB-B? Wait, which globins are adult-type in the axolotl? Axolotls are neotenic (paedomorphic), so they retain larval characteristics — do they express larval globins even as adults? Hmm. Axolotl globins: HBA1/HBD etc. are annotated as human orthologs. HB-AM and HB-B look like axolotl-specific names. For blood-richness markers, include all globins.\n- Stomach: PGA3, PGBC2/PGC, GKN1, GKN2, TFF2, CHGA, CHGB, MUC5AC, ATP4B, MUC1, ANXA4 (ANXA4 is more biliary but is also expressed in gastric mucosa).\n- Gallbladder: ANXA4, SLC4A2, KRT19, KRT8, KRT18, SOX9, MUC1, EPCAM, FOXA1, MUC5B? Hmm, MUC5B is also in the lung. Should I keep it.\n- Prostate: NKX3-1, MSMB, KLK1, KLK7, KLKB1, HOXB13, SLC4A2? No — SLC4A2 is a general one. Keep the first 6.\n- Kidney: SLC12A1, UMOD, UMODL1, SLC12A3, CALB1, NPHS1, NPHS2, LRP2, CUBN, PAX2, GATA3, SLC34A1! (NPT2a — top gene in the kidney column), PAX8 (top gene in the kidney column!), SLC23A3, SLC26A1, ACOT12. Adding SLC34A1 and PAX8 — strong proximal tubule markers. Hmm wait, PAX8 is also thyroid; the axolotl thyroid... well fine, PAX8+SLC34A1 is kidney.\n- Lung: SFTPC, NKX2-1, MUC5B, FOXA2, plus check the existence of SFTPD? SCGB3A1? Earlier check: SFTPA1/SFTPB are absent, SFTPC is present. OK.\n- Limb: keep, and maybe add PRRX1 (top gene in the limb column!). PRRX1 is a limb mesenchyme TF ✓. DCT is melanocyte (skin) ✓ reasonable.\n- Cloaca: VIL1, CDX2, UPK1B, UPK3A, KRT5, KRT13, FOXA2, TFF3.2! (TFF3 is intestinal/cloacal trefoil), MUC2? Cloaca receives hindgut — TFF3/MUC2 positive. Add TFF3.2, MUC2.\n- Intestine: VIL1, CDX2, FABP2, ANPEP, DPP4, SI, ALPI, MUC2, RBP2, FABP6, NXPE4? SLC15A1? CDHR2? Add NXPE4, SLC15A1.S, CDHR2 (top genes in the intestine column — but wait, adding \"genes I discovered in that column\" creates circular reasoning! Be careful. NXPE4, SLC15A1 (PEPT1? no, SLC15A1 = PEPT1 is intestinal peptide transporter ✓ known intestinal), CDHR2 is intestinal ✓, NXPE4 (neurexophilin/peptidase, gut?) — SLC15A1 and CDHR2 are legitimately known intestinal genes; NXPE4 is less canonical. Only add the ones with known biology: SLC15A1, CDHR2, TM4SF20? (TM4SF20 = liver/gut?) skip.\n- Spleen: add FLT3, RAG1, RAG2, CXCR5, CD72, POU2AF1? — these are all legitimate immune/B-cell markers ✓. Add.\n- Brain: no problem. Add STMN4? JAKMIP2? These are legitimate neuronal genes ✓ but unnecessary — the brain is already clean.\n- Liver: no problem (top genes are all liver).\n- Pancreas: no problem.\n- Heart: no problem.\n\nBe careful about circularity: the organ column's top genes confirmed known biology (e.g., kidney column → SLC34A1/PAX8 are known kidney genes — safe to add as markers since they are established markers). For the suspicious columns (stomach/gallbladder/prostate/bladder/gill), don't add from their own top genes; only add known markers.\n\nWait, but hmm: PAX8 — the kidney column's top gene; PAX8 is a known kidney (and thyroid) marker. Adding is OK.\n\nNext, the variance floor for z. Let's define z_ij = (x_ij - mean_i)/max(sd_i, s0). Here s0 = median of sd_i across all genes (with positive sd). Genes with zero counts everywhere → x is constant → excluded from marker averaging (nanmean).\n\nAlso, for robustness, compute two score variants:\n1. Mean z with variance floor.\n2. Rank-based / sign-based: for each marker gene of organ A, count the fraction of organ B's columns that exceed organ A's column (win rate). swap_score(A,B) = fraction of A's markers where z(B)>z(A) plus the same for B's markers → a value in [0,2]; for a true swap, it should be close to 2 (most markers move correctly). This is robust to outliers.\n\nLet's implement both, rank the pairs, and see which pair comes out on top."}}
