{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"reasoning","text":"Even using the variance floor, the top pairs are all clustered around the same organs: GallBladder↔Stomach (dsum 1.61), GallBladder↔Prostate (dsum 1.53, win 1.45), Cloaca↔Stomach, Prostate↔Stomach, Bladder↔Stomach...\n\nKey problem: the \"top pairs\" are dominated by one direction (the marker set of some organ X with a negative self-z finding a slightly better location). GallBladder's markers are consistently \"unhappy\" (self -0.69), and they improve in the Prostate column (+1.54) or Stomach column (+1.37) or Cloaca column (+1.57) — roughly the same improvement in three different places! Meanwhile, Prostate's markers don't improve at all in the GallBladder column (-0.01), and Stomach's markers only improve marginally in the GallBladder column (+0.24).\n\nThis pattern — GB's markers being similarly improved in Prostate/Stomach/Cloaca columns, without a reciprocal response — suggests either that the GallBladder column isn't really gallbladder tissue to begin with, or that the GB marker set is misjudging. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually — hmm, let me rethink this. What if the swap is **GallBladder ↔ Liver**? GB markers in Liver column: 0.07 (nope). Liver markers in GB column: 0.07? Liver self 2.08. Nope.\n\nWhat about **GallBladder ↔ Intestine**? GB→Intestine +1.02 (in the earlier un-floored version: GallBladder↔Intestine dsum -0.05, win 1.09)... GB markers improve in the Intestine column, but Intestine markers get worse in the GB column. Nope.\n\nHmm, let me look at the actual GB markers one by one in the Prostate column:\n- KRT19: Gill argmax 1.34, Prostate -0.06\n- KRT8: Cloaca 1.43, Prostate 0.84\n- KRT18: Cloaca 1.31, Prostate 1.18\n- SOX9: Cloaca 1.71, Prostate 1.20\n- ANXA4: Stomach 1.45, Prostate 0.56\n- SLC4A2: Prostate 3.00 (!!)\n- MUC1: Cloaca 1.80, Prostate 1.69\n- EPCAM: Gill 2.19, Prostate 1.29\n- FOXA1: Stomach 0.69, Prostate 0.62\n\nHmm, interesting — SLC4A2 z=3.00 in Prostate, MUC1 1.69, KRT18 1.18, SOX9 1.20, EPCAM 1.29. Multiple GB markers really are high in the Prostate column. But are these \"biliary\" genes really biliary? SLC4A2 (AE2) is nearly ubiquitous (erythrocytes, stomach parietal cells, kidney, liver canaliculi...). MUC1 is in many epithelia. KRT18 is a simple epithelial keratin (liver, gallbladder, intestine, urothelium, prostate...). SOX9 is in many tissues. EPCAM is in all epithelia. Only KRT19 and ANXA4 are reasonably biliary-specific.\n\nSo the GB marker set is mostly generic epithelial markers. The GB column itself has low values for generic epithelial markers (KRT8 -1.23, KRT18 -1.06, EPCAM -1.32) — meaning the GallBladder column has globally low epithelial accessibility?? Which would be odd for any organ... unless the GallBladder sample is dominated by non-epithelial tissue (stroma/muscle/blood), or is a low-quality/low-cellularity sample.\n\nHmm, wait, actually — what about the **Prostate column**? Its top genes are DMBT1, PARPI... DMBT1 is an epithelial mucin-like gene. And GB markers (KRT18, MUC1, SOX9, EPCAM, SLC4A2) are high in the Prostate column — the Prostate column looks like simple epithelium. Prostate is epithelial! So maybe the Prostate column is really... hmm, prostate markers themselves (NKX3-1, MSMB, KLK1, HOXB13) are low in the Prostate column. NKX3-1's argmax is Cloaca 1.78/Heart 1.72. HOXB13's argmax is Stomach 1.09.\n\nHmm, wait, wait. Let me think about this from a different angle: maybe the prostate markers are also unreliable in the axolotl (NKX3-1 has multiple homeodomain paralogs...).\n\n**What if the GallBladder column is actually Prostate tissue and the Prostate column is GallBladder tissue?** GB↔Prostate: win rate 1.45 (highest!), dsum 1.53. GB markers strongly improve in the Prostate column (+1.54), but Prostate markers show no improvement in the GB column (-0.01). The asymmetry bothers me. But if my Prostate marker set is bad (only 9 genes: NKX3-1, MSMB, KLK1, KLK7, KLKB1, HOXB13 — and KLK1 is nan, MSMB is weak), then the reverse-direction signal could be diluted. Hmm.\n\nLet me check what's actually open in the Prostate column's top genes and see if it looks biliary/prostate-like: PARPI_0000816, LOC103278924, LOC106962397, LOC102352752, DMBT1, LOC115089254, LOC113106536, LOC115475010, LOC101808314, DUI87_08714, LOC114557901, LOC116282477, DR999_PMT02662, LOC103173272, LOC102684809, LOC108696957, NRIP3, N323_02462, FANCA, CB1_000812029, H355_005836, LOC114595310.\n\nLet me look up the gene_name annotations for these — they might reveal the tissue program. Similarly for the GallBladder column's top genes and the Stomach column's top genes.\n\nAlso, let me reconsider the **Stomach column**: top genes are MUC2 (intestinal goblet cells!), CD8B (T cells), FFAR4, FMO1, FGF17. And Intestine markers have their second-highest value in the Stomach column (0.63). Stomach markers' argmax is Cloaca. Hmm, what if the Stomach column actually contains INTESTINE?? But the Intestine column itself looks genuinely intestinal (SI, NXPE4, CDHR2, FABP2...). Then Stomach↔Intestine? Intestine markers in the Stomach column 0.63 vs Intestine column 1.23 — nope.\n\nWhat if the Stomach column is CLOACA? Cloaca markers in the Stomach column: 0.60 vs Cloaca column 0.63 — comparable! And Stomach markers in the Cloaca column: -0.04 vs Stomach column -0.34. Hmm, weak support either way. Δ(Cloaca, Stomach) = 0.91, win 1.44. This is driven by Stomach markers improving in the Cloaca column (+0.95), while Cloaca markers stay flat (-0.04).\n\nHmm, okay. Another thought — maybe I'm being misled by the marker sets, and I should use an entirely different evidence type: **chromosome-level/bin-level similarity against expected organ biology is weak; instead use the GTF + ATAC to build an \"organ identity\" classifier via TF motif–like proxies?** Too heavy.\n\nLet me try looking at the gene_name annotations for the top genes in the suspicious columns and see what program they represent. I'll pull out the gene_name for about the top 50 genes of each suspicious column (Stomach, GallBladder, Prostate, Bladder, Gill, Cloaca, Kidney, Limb)."}}
