{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_79","type":"reasoning","text":"This is a treasure trove. Let me carefully assemble the strongest signals:\n\n**Uroplakins (bladder/urothelium program):**\n- UPK2.S: Gill 2.46 (argmax!), Stomach 1.16, Prostate 0.89, Bladder 0.69, Limb 0.49.\n- UPK1A: Gill 2.96 (argmax), Bladder 0.90, Stomach 0.73.\n- UPK1B: Gill 1.79 (argmax), Prostate 1.49, Bladder 1.32, Stomach 1.11.\n- UPK3A: Gill 2.21 (argmax), Cloaca 1.94, Heart 0.61, Prostate 0.51, Bladder 0.55.\n- UPK3B: Gill 2.60 (argmax), Stomach 0.57, Intestine 0.56.\n\nAll five uroplakins peak in the Gill column! This is extremely strong. Uroplakins are the most urothelium-specific gene family known. So the Gill column contains urothelium → the Gill column is probably the bladder!\n\nWait, but hold on. UPK3A is also 1.94 in Cloaca (the cloaca has a urothelial portion — reasonable). UPK1B is 1.49 in Prostate. Hmm. But the Gill column is the argmax for all five.\n\nIf the Gill column is the bladder, then the Bladder column should be the gills. Check gill markers in the Bladder column: ATP6V1B1 1.42 (2nd highest after Kidney 2.07!), RHCG 1.20 (2nd after Gill 1.98), SLC14A2? (not a gill marker), HBA1 -0.04, HB-AM -0.56, HB-B -0.53, CA2 0.14, UMOD 0.20... hmm, globins are negative in the Bladder column. AQP-T2 is 3.21 in the Bladder column (argmax) — aquaporin-T2, probably a kidney/urinary aquaporin...\n\nHmm wait, AQP-T2's argmax is the Bladder column (3.21). If the Bladder column is really the gills, AQP-T2 shouldn't peak there... unless AQP-T2 is expressed in the gills (aquaporins are involved in osmoregulation in fish/amphibian gills!). Hmm, actually — AQP3/AQP-T2-type aquaporins do exist in amphibian gills for water/ion transport. Hmm.\n\nAnd MYH3 (muscle myosin heavy chain — skeletal/embryonic) is 1.00 in the Bladder column. MYH3 in gills? The gills have skeletal muscle (branchial muscles)... hmm, possible.\n\nHmm, wait, let me check the UMOD family: UMOD's argmax is Gill 1.81! UMOD is the kidney loop of Henle marker Tamm-Horsfall protein. UMOD is also high in the Gill column. And the top genes of the Gill column include 3 UMOD paralogs + KRT4 + KRT76. KRT4's argmax is Gill 3.38; KRT76's argmax is Gill 3.34. KRT4/KRT76 are stratified epithelial keratins.\n\nHmm, hmm. So what tissue is: UPK1A/1B/3A/3B/2.S+, UMOD+, KRT4+, KRT76+, VTCN1+, FCGBP+, CNN2+, PLAUR+?\n\nUroplakins + keratins of stratified epithelium = urothelium (the bladder lining is a stratified epithelium expressing UPKs and KRT4/13/20-type keratins!). UMOD... hmm, uromodulin should be kidney. But wait — maybe the axolotl \"UMOD\" paralogs are not true uromodulin; it might be a ZP-domain gene family expanded in amphibian skin/gland secretions. Hmm.\n\nHmm wait, actually — hold on. What if the Gill column is not the bladder but the KIDNEY? Kidney = renal tubules (UMOD+ in the loop of Henle/distal tubule ✓) + urothelium of the renal pelvis/ureter (UPK+ ✓)! A whole-kidney sample contains both renal tubules and pelvic urothelium! But the Kidney column itself looks like a genuine kidney (SLC34A1 3.30, PAX8 2.87 argmax is Kidney; SLC12A1 1.56 argmax is Kidney). Two kidney columns?? Then what's in the Gill column... hmm, no.\n\nAlternatively, the Gill column = bladder (UPK+), and UMOD/KRT4/KRT76 in the Gill column: KRT4/KRT76 ✓ urothelial-type keratins, UMOD paralogs might be misannotated ZP genes expressed in the urothelium (ZP-module proteins are abundant in... hmm, actually in mammals the urothelial plaque is made of uroplakins which have ZP domains!). UPK3A/UPK2/UPK3B are all ZP-domain proteins. The axolotl \"UMOD\" paralogs might actually be uroplakin-like ZP genes! LOC115073438 etc. — hmm, they were annotated as \"UMOD [hs]\" but they might be UPK-family. Plausible!\n\nSo: **the Gill column ≈ bladder/urothelium tissue.** Strong evidence: all five UPKs have argmax in Gill + KRT4/KRT76 (stratified/urothelial keratins).\n\nNext, the counterpart: the Bladder column should be the gills. Gill program = respiration + ion regulation + blood: globins, CA, Rh, ion transporters. In the Bladder column: ATP6V1B1 1.42 (V-ATPase — ionocyte marker!), RHCG 1.20 (Rhcg — ammonia transport in gill ionocytes!), AQP-T2 3.21, MYH3 1.00, STAR 0.77, MUC5B 0.87, TFF2 1.42 (!), SLC14A2 0.37...\n\nHmm, TFF2 (trefoil factor 2, gastric) is 1.42 in the Bladder column — its argmax. And TFF2 is 0.60 in the Gill column. TFF2 is a gastric marker... why is it in the gills? Axolotl gills don't have TFF2. Hmm, but hmm, TFF peptides are mucosal protective factors; TFF2/XR (xenopus-related?) — amphibian TFFs are expressed in stomach and skin/mucosal tissues. Hmm, actually in Xenopus, TFF2 is stomach-specific. Hmm.\n\nBut globins are negative in the Bladder column (HBA1 -0.04, HB-AM -0.56, HB-B -0.53, HBD? let me check... HBD in Bladder is -0.53). Gill tissue should be rich in blood → globin-high. But the Gill column's globins are only moderately high (HBA1 1.19, HB-AM 0.69, HB-B 0.77) — not overwhelming. Meanwhile the Lung column's globins are huge (HB-AM 2.29, HB-B 2.28, HBD 2.28, HBA1 1.45). Hmm.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me think about the Lung column's globins again. SFTPC 2.91 argmax Lung, NKX2-1 1.03 argmax Lung — the Lung column is definitely lung tissue. Lung globins are high — because the lung is perfused with blood, so there's a lot of blood in the lung sample. OK.\n\nIf the Bladder column is the gills, why are globins low? Gill vasculature: the gills receive the entire venous output... gills should be blood-rich. Hmm. But the blood content of the sample is variable (perfusion state). Weak evidence either way.\n\nATP6V1B1: argmax is Kidney 2.07, then Bladder 1.42, Prostate 1.58. V-ATPase B1 subunit — kidney intercalated cells + epididymis/prostate?? Hmm, actually ATP6V1B1 is expressed in kidney intercalated cells and epididymis; mutations cause dRTA. Bladder 1.42... hmm.\n\nRHCG: argmax is Gill 1.98, Prostate 2.22, Bladder 1.20. RhCG — kidney intercalated cells + epididymis/prostate testis! Hmm, RHCG is high in the Prostate column (2.22). CA2: argmax is Prostate 2.92! CA2 (carbonic anhydrase 2) in the prostate?? CA2 is expressed in kidney intercalated cells, erythrocytes, and... prostate? Hmm, actually CA2 is reported in prostatic epithelium. Hmm.\n\nWait — the Prostate column has high CA2 (2.92), RHCG (2.22), SLC4A2 (3.00), ATP6V1B1 (1.58), SLC12A3 (1.69), PAX2 (2.46), SLC14A2 -0.35, GATA3 (1.19). CA2+RHCG+SLC4A2+ATP6V1B1 = intercalated cell program (kidney collecting duct)! And PAX2 (kidney developmental gene) 2.46. SLC12A3 (DCT marker) 1.69. Hmm!! The Prostate column looks like the distal nephron/collecting duct of the kidney!!\n\nBut the Kidney column has proximal tubule (SLC34A1 3.30, PAX8 2.87, SLC22A6, SLC23A3, SLC26A1, ACOT12, AKR1A1, SLC47A1, GRHPR — all proximal tubule!). Hmm, so the Kidney column is proximal tubule-dominant and the Prostate column is distal nephron-dominant? Both could be \"kidney\" but with different regional compositions... that's odd for two separate organs.\n\nHmm, wait, or the Prostate column = kidney, and the Kidney column = prostate?? No — the Kidney column's top genes are definitively proximal tubule (SLC34A1 is extremely PT-specific).\n\nHmm, OK, wait, let me step back and think about which organs could plausibly be swapped given the anatomy and design:\n\nCandidate A: **Gill ↔ Bladder**.\n- Gill column: UPK+ (5/5 uroplakins argmax), KRT4/KRT76+ → urothelium ✓✓ (bladder-like).\n- Bladder column: ATP6V1B1+, RHCG+, AQP-T2+, TFF2+, MUC5B+, MYH3+, STAR+... hmm, is that gill? ATP6V1B1+RHCG are ionocyte/ammonia transport markers (gill-like ✓). AQP-T2: aquaporin — gills do express aquaporins for osmoregulation ✓ plausible. MYH3: skeletal muscle — gill arch muscles ✓ plausible. TFF2: hmm, weird for gills, but amphibian TFFs might be mucosal. STAR: weird.\n\nCandidate B: **Stomach ↔ Cloaca**.\n- Cloaca column: GKN1 2.15, GKN2 2.19, CLDN18.S 3.62 (argmax!), MUC1 1.80, CHGA 0.71, ANXA4 1.10, TFF3.2 3.37, PGC 0.57 → gastric program (GKN1/2, CLDN18!) ✓✓.\n- Stomach column: MUC2 3.3, TFF3.2 0.50, CDX2 2.39 (!!), VIL1 1.40, FOXA2 1.00, UPK1B 1.11, SLC14A2 -2.12, CALB1 2.33, CD8B 3.19, FMO1 3.20, FFAR4 3.11 → hmm, CDX2 2.39 in the Stomach column! CDX2 is the master TF of intestinal identity. High CDX2 + MUC2 + VIL1 + CALB1 (intestinal calcium absorption!) + FFAR4 + CD8B (intraepithelial lymphocytes!) = intestinal/cloacal program ✓✓.\n\nWow. CDX2 2.39 in the Stomach column — that's huge. CDX2 is definitively intestinal. And the Cloaca column has GKN1/GKN2/CLDN18 — definitively gastric.\n\nHmm, wait, but — CDX2 in the Cloaca column is only 0.59, and in the Intestine column 2.16 (argmax = Intestine ✓). VIL1: Intestine 0.69, Stomach 1.40 (argmax!), Cloaca 1.14. CALB1: Intestine 2.12, Stomach 2.33 (argmax). Hmm, so the Stomach column has CDX2 2.39 > Intestine column 2.16, CALB1 2.33 > Intestine 2.12, VIL1 1.40 > Intestine 0.69... but the Intestine column has CDHR2/SI/NXPE4/SLC15A1/FABP2 (brush border absorptive program) as its argmax.\n\nSo: Intestine column = absorptive enterocyte program (SI, CDHR2, NXPE4, SLC15A1, FABP2, ANPEP, DPP4 — all argmax in Intestine? Let me verify: SI argmax is Intestine 3.3 ✓...). Stomach column = goblet/secretory intestinal program (MUC2, CDX2, CALB1, VIL1, FFAR4, CD8B). Hmm — these are both intestinal programs but different cell-type compositions! The Stomach column could be either (a) a different segment of the gut (cloaca/hindgut with more goblet cells), or (b) a different intestinal region.\n\nAnd the Cloaca column has gastric GKN1/GKN2/CLDN18 + TFF3.2 (intestinal-type trefoil) + MUC1 — mixed gastric + intestinal?? The cloaca is a common chamber: it receives the hindgut (intestinal program) + urothelium + gastric glands?? Hmm, actually, the distal axolotl gut... hmm.\n\nWait, wait. Let me think. If **Stomach ↔ Cloaca** were swapped:\n- The Cloaca column should be stomach tissue: GKN1 ✓, GKN2 ✓, CLDN18 ✓ (3.62!), CHGA ✓ (0.71), MUC1 ✓ (1.80), ANXA4 ✓ (1.10), PGC ✓ (0.57), PGA3 (0.10 — weak), TFF2 (-0.55 ✗), ATP4B (-2.17 ✗✗), MUC5AC (nan→0.23), CHGB (0.98).\n- The Stomach column should be cloaca tissue: CDX2 ✓ (2.39!), MUC2 ✓ (3.3!), VIL1 ✓ (1.40), FOXA2 ✓ (1.00), TFF3.2 ✓ (0.50), UPK1B ✓ (1.11 — urothelial portion of the cloaca!), UPK3A (-0.18), UPK2.S (1.16!), KRT5 (0.13), KRT13 (0.57), SLC14A2 (-2.12 ✗ hmm).\n\nInteresting. The gastric evidence in the Cloaca column is very strong (GKN1 2.15, GKN2 2.19, CLDN18.S 3.62 — GKN1/2 and CLDN18 are among the most stomach-specific genes known). The cloacal evidence in the Stomach column is also strong (CDX2 2.39, MUC2 3.3, VIL1 1.40, UPK2.S 1.16, UPK1B 1.11, TFF3.2 0.50, FOXA2 1.00).\n\nThe contradictions: TFF2 and ATP4B (parietal cell markers) are negative in the Cloaca column; but hmm, TFF2's argmax is Bladder 1.42 / Heart 1.28 — TFF2 behaves weirdly overall (its \"expected\" location, the stomach, is -2.45 in the Stomach column). Hmm wait, TFF2 is -2.45 in the Stomach column and -0.55 in the Cloaca column — under the swap hypothesis, TFF2 should be high in the Cloaca column (stomach tissue). -0.55 is not high. ✗. And ATP4B: -2.17 in Cloaca ✗✗. Hmm. Parrietals... maybe the axolotl stomach has few parietal cells? Actually — amphibian stomach: do amphibians have parietal cells? Amphibians secrete HCl; they have oxyntic cells (parietal-equivalent) that express ATP4A/ATP4B. Hmm. But ATP4B's z-vector: argmax is Bladder 1.33, Kidney 1.01, Pancreas 0.94, Heart 0.88, Gill -1.49, Cloaca -2.17, Stomach -1.12. Strange pattern — ATP4B might not be a reliable marker here (paralog confusion).\n\nLet me also check the TFF2 paralog issue: sym2prim['TFF2'] = {'AB205_0205010','AB205_0020180'} — hmm, those are nr IDs, might be misannotated. And TFF3.2 is a separate gene. OK.\n\nSo for Stomach↔Cloaca: strong positive evidence (GKN1/2, CLDN18 in the Cloaca column; CDX2, MUC2, VIL1, UPK2.S, UPK1B, TFF3.2, FOXA2 in the Stomach column), some contradictions (TFF2, ATP4B — both single genes with paralog annotation problems).\n\nNow compare with Gill↔Bladder:\n- Gill column: UPK1A 2.96, UPK2.S 2.46, UPK3B 2.60, UPK3A 2.21, UPK1B 1.79 (all argmax), KRT4 3.38, KRT76 3.34, UMOD 1.81, VTCN1, FCGBP, CNN2 → urothelium ✓✓✓.\n- Bladder column: gill program? ATP6V1B1 1.42, RHCG 1.20, AQP-T2 3.21, MYH3 1.00, STAR 0.77, TFF2 1.42, MUC5B 0.87, TMPRSS13, B4GALNT2... globins are negative. Hmm, weaker/more ambiguous.\n\nHmm! Both candidate pairs have strong evidence in one direction and ambiguous evidence in the other. Ugh.\n\nBut wait — wait. Let me reconsider. Could both swaps be present? No — the task says two organ labels are swapped (a single pair).\n\nLet me think about which direction is more reliable. The evidence that the Cloaca column contains gastric tissue is very strong: GKN1 (z 2.15), GKN2 (2.19), CLDN18 (3.62) — GKN1/GKN2 (gastrokines) are essentially 100% stomach-specific in vertebrates, and CLDN18 is a gastric claudin (CLDN18.1/2 are stomach-specific). Their argmax is the Cloaca column, and the Stomach column is -0.63/0.11/-0.50. This is decisive: the tissue in the Cloaca column is stomach.\n\nThe evidence that the Gill column contains urothelium is also very strong: five uroplakins all argmax in Gill (z 1.79-2.96), plus KRT4/KRT76. Decisive: the tissue in the Gill column is urothelial (bladder/cloacal urothelium).\n\nNow, if only one pair is swapped, then either:\n(a) Cloaca ↔ Stomach (the Cloaca column is stomach; the Stomach column is cloaca) — in this case, the Gill column is... genuine gills expressing uroplakins?? That's biologically implausible — gills don't express uroplakins.\n(b) Gill ↔ Bladder (the Gill column is bladder; the Bladder column is gills) — in this case, the Cloaca column is... genuine cloaca expressing GKN1/GKN2/CLDN18 at z 2-3.6?? That's biologically implausible — the cloaca doesn't have gastric glands... hmm wait, actually — hmm!\n\nHmm wait. Let me think about axolotl anatomy again. The larval/juvenile axolotl gut: esophagus → stomach → intestine → ... cloaca. The stomach is clearly demarcated. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait — but hold on. What if the \"Cloaca\" sample actually contains adjacent stomach tissue? No...\n\nLet me think more carefully about (b): if Gill↔Bladder is the swap, then the Cloaca column = genuine cloaca. Would the genuine axolotl cloaca express GKN1/GKN2/CLDN18/MUC1/CHGA/ANXA4/PGC? That would mean the cloaca has gastric-type epithelium. Hmm, unlikely.\n\nUnder (a): Stomach↔Cloaca is the swap → Cloaca column = genuine stomach tissue ✓ (GKN1/2, CLDN18 ✓✓✓); Stomach column = genuine cloaca ✓ (CDX2, MUC2, VIL1, TFF3.2, UPK1B/UPK2.S — the urothelial component of the cloaca ✓, KRT13 ✓). Then the Gill column = genuine gills?? But genuine gills shouldn't express five uroplakins at z 1.8-3.0!! Unless... hmm.\n\nHmm, hmm. However... wait. Let me re-verify: are those UPK genes really annotated as uroplakins? UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK2.S, UPK3A, UPK3B — yes, they're uroplakin symbols. Hmm.\n\nCould the axolotl gills express uroplakin-like genes? Gill epithelium... ionocytes... hmm, actually there's a subtle possibility: the \"UPK\" annotations might include broader ZP-domain family members. But UPK1A/UPK1B/UPK3A/UPK2/UPK3B are all named as uroplakins in the annotation. Five independent gene families all peaking in the Gill column — that's real signal.\n\nHmm, wait — what about the UPK1B in the Prostate column (1.49)? UPK2.S in the Stomach column (1.16)? UPK3A in the Cloaca column (1.94)? These are all \"urothelium-adjacent\" organs (prostate ducts are lined with urothelium-like epithelium, cloacal urothelium ✓, stomach ✗ weird...).\n\nHmm, OK, wait, actually — let me reconsider the Stomach column = cloaca hypothesis with uroplakins: cloacal urothelium → UPK1B 1.11, UPK2.S 1.16, UPK1A 0.73, UPK3A -0.18, UPK3B 0.57 ✓ plausible.\nAnd the Gill column = bladder: UPK1A 2.96, UPK2.S 2.46, UPK3B 2.60, UPK3A 2.21, UPK1B 1.79 ✓✓✓.\nThe Bladder column = gills: globins negative ✗, but ATP6V1B1 1.42, RHCG 1.20 ✓ ionocytes; AQP-T2 3.21 ✓ aquaporin (osmoregulation).\nThe Cloaca column = genuine cloaca: GKN1 2.15, GKN2 2.19, CLDN18 3.62?? — gastric markers in the genuine cloaca?? ✗✗✗.\n\nSo (b) fails on the Cloaca column's gastric program. And (a) fails on the Gill column's urothelial program. Contradiction!!\n\nHowever... hmm. Wait. Let me recheck CLDN18.S and GKN1/2. CLDN18.S — the \".S\" suffix suggests a short isoform; maybe the primary CLDN18 gene is elsewhere. CLDN18.S and CLDN18 have the same z-vector (both listed, identical). OK.\n\nHmm, wait a moment, what if the swapped pair is **Stomach ↔ Gill**?? Let's check: the Gill column is stomach? GKN1 in Gill is 0.24, GKN2 -0.05, CLDN18 0.01, PGA3 0.13 — no. ✗.\n\nWhat if the pair is **Cloaca ↔ Gill**? The Gill column is cloaca? UPK ✓ (cloacal urothelium), KRT4/KRT76 ✓ stratified epithelium, TFF3.2 in Gill -0.12 ✗, GKN1 in Gill 0.24 ✗... and the Cloaca column is gills? HBA1 in Cloaca 1.20 ✓!, HB-AM 0.62, HB-B 0.02, CA2 -0.69, CA4 1.54 ✓!, RHCG -1.01 ✗, ATP6V1B1 -0.47 ✗, UMOD -0.05... mixed. Hmm, no.\n\nOK, hmm. Let me think about this differently. What if there's a third possibility: **the Gill column = bladder, and the Bladder column = cloaca, and the Cloaca column = stomach, and the Stomach column = ...** no — it's a single transposition of two labels, not a cycle.\n\nLet me re-derive from first principles. If exactly one pair (X,Y) is swapped, then exactly two columns are mislabeled, and all other columns are genuine. The genuine columns should have self-consistent marker profiles:\n- Brain ✓, Heart ✓, Liver ✓, Lung ✓, Pancreas ✓, Spleen ✓, Intestine ✓, Kidney ✓ (SLC34A1/PAX8 argmax Kidney), Limb ✓ (top genes are weak but PRRX1/DCT are plausible).\n\nThat leaves five suspicious columns: Stomach, Cloaca, Gill, Bladder, Prostate, GallBladder — wait, six. And GallBladder: GB markers argmax is Prostate (0.85); GB column top genes: CYP8B1 (1.38 in GB — bile acid synthesis, liver/biliary ✓), ACP2, CP (2.6)... CYP8B1's argmax