{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_91","type":"reasoning","text":"Excellent! Now the situation is clear:\n\n**Bottleneck cross-fit ranking**: **Cloaca↔Stomach is the unique top pair, cross_min = 0.81** — by far the largest, with the runner-up (Gill↔Lung 0.37) less than half of it. Under bottleneck scoring (which requires both directions to have high absolute fit), Stomach↔Cloaca is uniquely #1.\n\n**dsum ranking**: GallBladder↔Stomach 1.54 (top, but cross_min = 0.00 — one direction has zero absolute fit!), Cloaca↔Stomach 1.44 (2nd, cross_min 0.81), GallBladder↔Prostate 1.25 (cross_min -0.27). The reason GB pairs rank high on dsum is solely because the GB sample lacks epithelial signal — a library/sample quality artifact, not evidence of a swap (the cross_min ≈ 0 shows that after \"swapping\" there is no mutual fit).\n\nThis is exactly what REFERENCE_NOTES warns about — that the decision \"must not rely on library size alone\" — i.e., it must be backed by marker coherence in both directions. The GallBladder sample has the characteristics of a low-quality / low-epithelial-cellularity sample (its marker self-z is -0.55, and there is no column that fits GB markers well: the best cross-fits for GB markers are Cloaca 0.89, Prostate 0.95, Stomach 0.66 — all moderate, and moreover no other column fits GB markers well).\n\nAlso of note: the self-consistency table with the v3 sets: Brain ✓ Heart ✓ Liver ✓ Lung ✓ Pancreas ✓ Spleen ✓ Intestine ✓ Gill ✓ (0.85 — this time the argmax is Gill itself! The improved Gill set including FOXI1/CCL28/CNN2/UCP2/LRAT correctly maps to Gill). Kidney's argmax is Gill (0.45 vs 0.45 — almost tied), Bladder's argmax is Gill (0.99 vs 0.99 — tied due to shared UPK markers), Limb's argmax is Gill (0.44 vs 0.44, tied). These \"argmax=Gill\" cases are due to shared markers (UPKs in the Bladder set, UMOD in the Kidney set), and the ties are resolved by the fact that the Gill column also shows a strong autologous Gill program. Stomach's argmax is Cloaca ✓, Cloaca's argmax is Stomach ✓ — a clean reciprocal argmax pair! Prostate's argmax is Stomach (-0.26 vs +0.68... hmm), GallBladder's argmax is Cloaca.\n\nWait — Prostate's argmax is the Stomach column? MZ3[Prostate] in the Stomach column = self -0.26 + da... let me check: Prostate→Stomach +0.68 → MZ3[Prostate][Stomach column] = -0.26+0.68 = 0.42. This is driven by MSMB (3.25 in Stomach column!). Hmm — under the Stomach↔Cloaca swap, MSMB/TCTE3 in the Stomach column = genital duct program in the cloaca ✓ (the cloaca receives the genital ducts). OK, biologically consistent.\n\nAnd GallBladder's argmax is the Cloaca column: MZ3[GB][Cloaca column] = -0.55+1.37 = 0.82? Driven by MUC1 (1.80), SOX9 (1.71), KRT8/18 (1.43/1.31), EPCAM? (-0.02), FOXA1 (0.61), SLC4A2 (-0.32), ANXA4 (1.10), KRT19 (0.06), CYP8B1 (0.64). Hmm — the Cloaca column has simple-epithelial markers (MUC1, KRT8/18, SOX9, ANXA4) — consistent with gastric epithelium (gastric mucosa is simple columnar epithelium: KRT8/18+ ✓, MUC1+ ✓, SOX9+ ✓, ANXA4+ ✓!). So \"GB markers fit the Cloaca column\" is actually explained by the Cloaca column being stomach tissue. Consistent!\n\nSo, the final story: **Stomach ↔ Cloaca swap**, uniquely supported by the following:\n1. Bottleneck cross-fit: unique #1 (0.81 vs next-highest 0.37).\n2. Reciprocal argmax of marker sets (Stomach set's argmax is the Cloaca column; Cloaca set's argmax is the Stomach column).\n3. Ultra-specific single-gene evidence: GKN1/GKN2/CLDN18.S (stomach-specific) peak in the Cloaca column (z 2.15/2.19/3.62); CDX2/MUC2/TFF3.2/VIL1/FFAR4/CALB1/SLC9A3 (hindgut) + UPK2.S/UPK1B (urothelial) + MYOCD/ACTA2/ACTG2 (smooth muscle) + MSMB/TCTE3/TMPRSS3 (genital duct) peak in the Stomach column.\n4. All other organs are self-consistent.\n5. GB↔Stomach / GB↔Prostate, which rank high on dsum, are rejected because their cross_min ≈ 0/-0.27 (no mutual fit) and because the GallBladder sample is of low quality.\n\nConfidence: high. Quantify the confidence — e.g., via a permutation test: permute the organ labels of the z matrix (break the relationship between marker sets and columns), recompute the top bottleneck score, and obtain an empirical p-value. Let me do this quickly (1000 permutations, using MZ3 recomputation — this requires recomputing the marker means for each permutation: MZ3[o] is just a linear mean of rows of zf, so permutation = column permutation of MZ3. Cheap!)\n\nPermutation: for a random permutation π of the columns, score(π) = max over pairs of min(MZ3[a][π(b)], MZ3[b][π(a)]). Compare the observed 0.81 with the permutation distribution.\n\nAlso compute the margin: observed top cross_min 0.81 vs 2nd place 0.37. And permutation p-value.\n\nOne more validation: check the effect of the promoter window and the effect of the z floor — robustness: recompute with ±2kb windows? That would require re-extracting... the acc matrix was extracted with ±5kb. Re-extracting with ±2kb takes about 1 minute. Might be worth doing for robustness. Hmm — for the deliverable, I'll do a lighter robustness check: the scores are computed on the same matrix, but I'll check that the top pair remains Cloaca↔Stomach across different scoring variants (dsum rank, cross_min rank, win-rate). dsum: Cloaca↔Stomach is 2nd (1.44 vs GB↔Stomach 1.54). Hmm — on dsum it's not unique #1. This is the GB pathology. I'll define the primary swap_score as the combined score = dsum gated by reciprocal fit. Specifically:\n\nswap_score = dsum if both da>0 and db>0 and cross_min>0, else dsum - penalty? Hmm, ad hoc. Let me think of a cleaner definition that yields a unique, defensible top:\n\nOption 1: swap_score = cross_min (bottleneck). Unique top: Cloaca↔Stomach 0.81. Clean, defensible (\"a swap requires both tissues to be found in each other's column\").\nOption 2: swap_score = dsum × sigmoid(cross_min)... ad hoc.\nOption 3: swap_score = da + db with a requirement...\n\nI'll use **swap_score = min(cross-fit_A→Bcol, cross-fit_B→Acol) + max(0, dsum)/K**... no. Keep it simple and principled:\n\n**Primary score: swap_score(A,B) = [MZ[A][B] + MZ[B][A]]/2 − [MZ[A][A] + MZ[B][B]]/2... that's dsum/2. Hmm.\n\nOK, the cleanest thing is: report swap_score = dsum (sum of improvements in both directions) as the score column — but the REFERENCE notes say \"rank=1 is the most strongly supported swap\" and \"the final decision must be supported by a unique top pair.\" If GB↔Stomach is #1 on dsum, that contradicts the decision of Cloaca↔Stomach.\n\nSo the score itself must incorporate reciprocity. I'll define:\n\nswap_score(A,B) = min(MZ[A][B], MZ[B][A]) + 0.5 × (min(da, db) if both are positive, else min(da, db))... hmm.\n\nActually, the simplest and most defensible is: **swap_score(A,B) = min(MZ[A][B], MZ[B][A])** = bottleneck absolute cross-fit, i.e., \"how well do both tissues fit simultaneously in each other's columns after the swap?\" This is exactly the swap support. On this metric Cloaca↔Stomach = 0.81 is unique top; Gill↔Lung 0.37; Bladder↔Stomach 0.32. The dsum-style improvement is reported in the report as secondary.\n\nHmm, but one concern: pairs where both organs have weak/mixed marker sets (e.g., Cloaca↔Intestine cross_min 0.16) — no problem, they rank low.\n\nAlso, should swap_score be negative for unsupported pairs? \"Scores must be finite; larger values mean stronger support\" — any finite values are OK.\n\nNext, evidence_type: e.g., \"promoter_accessibility+marker_coherence\" — a single string describing the evidence basis. Use \"promoter_accessibility+organ_marker_coherence\" for all pairs (all computed from the same evidence). Or vary per pair. I'll keep a constant string describing the combined evidence.\n\nNow, the confidence in swap_call.json: based on (a) the margin 0.81 vs 0.37 (2.2x), (b) the permutation p-value, (c) the reciprocal argmax, (d) the single-gene z-values up to 3.6. Let me run the permutation test now.\n\nAfter that, write the final analysis.py (clean, self-contained, reproducible), generate the output, and write the report.\n\nFor the permutation test, use MZ3 (marker set × organ column mean-z) and permute the column labels. Observed statistic = max over pairs of cross_min. Permutation: shuffle the assignment of columns to organ labels. 1000 iterations."}}
