{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_100","type":"reasoning","text":"Robustness confirmed: across promoter windows ±2kb, ±5kb, ±10kb, **Cloaca↔Stomach is always ranked #1**, and the bottleneck cross-fit is consistently around 0.78–0.81, with a win-rate of 1.41–1.50.\n\nNext, I'll create the final deliverables. Structure:\n\n`output/analysis.py` — a self-contained script that does the following:\n1. Parse the GTF → gene TSS + symbol map.\n2. Build a contig→arm coordinate map from chrom.sizes.\n3. Stream-process the ATAC bin table and compute promoter accessibility per gene (overlap-weighted, per-bp CPM).\n4. Normalize (CPM → log2 → per-gene z with variance floor).\n5. Define organ marker sets; compute marker coherence matrix.\n6. For all 105 pairs: compute swap_score = cross_min + (win_rate − 1), plus auxiliary statistics; rank them.\n7. Write out output/sample_similarity.csv and output/swap_call.json; print a summary.\n\nCSV columns: organ_a, organ_b, swap_score, rank, evidence_type. Lexicographic order within rows (use sorted(ORGS) and itertools.combinations → already lexicographic). rank is 1..105, with 1 = highest score. evidence_type: \"promoter_accessibility+organ_marker_coherence\".\n\nswap_call.json:\n{\n  \"swap_detected\": true,\n  \"organ_a\": \"Cloaca\",\n  \"organ_b\": \"Stomach\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"evidence\": [...list of evidence strings...]\n}\n\nWait — organ_a/organ_b: lexicographic order \"Cloaca\" < \"Stomach\" ✓.\n\nAlso, regarding the runtime of the final script: full pipeline is about 1–2 minutes. No problem.\n\nOne detail: the script computes libsize from the data itself (sum of bins) — better to compute than to hardcode.\n\nAnother detail: handling of `nan` genes in win-rate (excluded), and finiteness of scores — cross_min may end up taking the nanmean of an all-nan slice... marker sets always have some valid genes, so no problem. But guard against it: if the cross value is nan → set it to a large negative finite value (-10). \n\nReport.md — a comprehensive writeup: task, data, methods, results, evidence, alternative hypotheses considered and rejected, caveats, conclusion. Include tables of key z values.\n\nSo, I'll write analysis.py now. I'll write it directly to output/analysis.py, run it, and then write report.md.\n"}}
