h primary 'MUC5AC' (gene_name \"MUC5AC\") and 13 others with MUC5AC as a secondary hs symbol. The work_loci output for MUC5AC was truncated (\"n=14\" was partially visible). OK. So why did step12's vz('MUC5AC') show nan??\n\nLet me look at step12's code: `idxs = [sym2idx[p] for p in sym2prim.get(m, ()) if p in sym2idx]; if not idxs: return None; return np.nanmean(z[idxs], axis=0)`. If all 14 rows are individually nan → nanmean → nan (with a warning). All 14 rows being nan means all 14 genes are sd=0… but primary MUC5AC is not sd=0 (it has values here). Contradiction!!\n\nUnless the z in work_z.pkl and the one used in step12 differ… both loaded from work_z.pkl. Hmm, wait — step12 printed a row for MUC5AC: \"MUC5AC nan nan nan...\". Let me look at the exact earlier output… From the step12 output:\n\n\"MUC5AC nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan nan\" — yes, it was printed.\n\nAnd now: \"MUC5AC Stomach=-0.04 Cloaca=+2.63 argmax=Cloaca(2.63)\". Same pickle?! Weird. Oh wait. I got it — in step12, the MUC5AC query was inside a marker list… hmm no. Hmm, let me look at step12's output again… actually, was that MUC5AC row from the first big table in scratch_step12? The output showed \"MUC5AC nan ...\" — hmm, but actually the rows I saw were: \"MUC2 0.83 -0.15 0.09...\", \"TFF3.2...\", \"TFF2...\", \"PGA3...\", \"PGC...\", \"PGBC2 nan...\"… and later \"MUC5AC nan...\"?? Let me scroll through memory… the step12 output had:\n\n```\nMUC5AC         nan      nan ... (all nan)\n```\n\nHmm, wait, actually no! Looking at the step12 output more carefully — that row might have been for the 'MUC5AC' marker, but… hmm, I really do see \"MUC5AC nan nan nan...\" in the truncated output. But hmm, that might have been 'MUC6' (which I included in the query list — MUC6 doesn't exist in axolotl → vz returns None → \"MISSING\" was printed, not nan)…\n\nWell whatever — the values here are from the same work_z.pkl that the scratch analysis used, and the final analysis.py recomputed everything independently with consistent results. What matters for the report is: at the marker-set level, the Stomach marker set (including MUC5AC averaged over paralogs) fits the Cloaca column (mean z ~ +1.0) far better than the Stomach column (-0.35). The individual primary MUC5AC gene peaks in the Cloaca column (2.63) — consistent with gastric metaplasia… MUC5AC is a gastric surface mucin ✓.\n\nHmm OK wait, but one thing to be careful about: the numbers I cite in the report must match what analysis.py computed (marker-set level). The evidence statement in swap_call.json says \"GKN1/GKN2/CLDN18.S peaks in the Cloaca column (z~2.2-3.6)\" — at the marker level: GKN1 avg 2.15, GKN2 avg 2.19, CLDN18.S 3.62 ✓ (roughly 2.2-3.6 ✓). And \"CDX2/MUC2/FFAR4/CALB1/MSMB/TCTE3/UPK peaks in the Stomach column\": marker-level CDX2 (=CDX1) 2.39 ✓, MUC2 average -0.26 ✗ hmm — at the marker-average level MUC2 does not peak in the Stomach column! But the individual MUC2 gene does (3.34). The evidence string in the JSON cites MUC2 — at the individual ortholog level this is correct (the true MUC2 locus peaks in the Stomach column). The report should clarify this nuance: symbol-average dilution due to the MUC2/CDX2 paralogs; the score uses the conservative average and is still rank 1.\n\nSimilarly, the MSMB marker: average is 1.07 in Stomach column ✓ (LOC104073169 = 3.25). TCTE3 3.25 ✓ (single gene, but hmm — TCTE3 isn't in my marker set; it's additional supporting evidence).\n\nOK. Also confirming: FOXA2's argmax is Cloaca 2.37; ISL1's argmax is Cloaca 2.09; GATA4/GATA6's argmax is Heart but Cloaca 1.51/1.74 — consistent with the Cloaca column being a hollow organ with a muscular wall + endodermal epithelium (stomach ✓).\n\nAnd ALB in the Cloaca column is 1.48 — hmm, ALB's argmax is Liver 2.62, but 1.48 in the Cloaca column? The liver marker set in the Cloaca column = -0.14 (no problem, ALB alone is within acceptable noise).\n\nOne more consistency check against the numbers in the evidence: \"cross-fit z = 1.00 and 0.81\" ✓ (MZ[Cloaca][Stomach]=1.00, MZ[Stomach][Cloaca]=0.81).\n\nOK let me write report.md. Structure:\n\n# ATAC-seq sample swap detection report — Axolotl organ panel\n\n1. Summary / call\n2. Input data and preprocessing\n   - Count table (2,430,700 bins × 15 organs), library sizes\n   - Coordinate reconstruction contig→arm (validated)\n   - GTF (99,088 genes; 22,695 with symbols)\n3. Method\n   - Promoter matrix (TSS±5kb, overlap-weighted, CPM, log2)\n   - Variance-floor z-score\n   - Marker sets (curated, references to known organ biology via human ortholog symbols)\n   - Pairwise scores: dsum, cross_min, win_rate, swap_score = cross_min + (win_rate-1)\n   - Random-gene permutation\n4. Results\n   - Self-consistency table (argmax)\n   - Top-pair table (top 8)\n   - Decisive gene evidence table (marker-level z)\n   - Statistics: z=7.28, p<1e-4; robust to 2-10kb windows\n5. Alternative hypotheses evaluated and rejected\n   - GallBladder↔Stomach (dsum leader): one-directional; GB column lacks epithelial program (low cellularity/quality); cross_min 0.00; no gastric markers in GB column; not unique.\n   - GallBladder↔Prostate: cross_min -0.27, one-directional.\n   - Gill↔Bladder: UPK signal in Gill column considered; rejected: Gill column retains genuine gill program (FOXI1, DLX5, MSX2, CCL28, CNN2), Bladder column lacks gill program (globins negative; win-rate 0.65; cross_min -0.05); UPK/KRT4/KRT76/GRHL3 in Gill consistent with barrier/epidermal-type epithelium of amphibian gill; Bladder column consistent with genuine bladder (AQP-T2 3.21, detrusor smooth muscle MYOCD program, moderate UPK).\n   - Prostate↔Stomach / Prostate↔Kidney: distal nephron-like ion-transport program (CA2, SLC4A2, RHCG, ATP6V1B1) in Prostate column consistent with Wolffian duct / male reproductive duct (epididymal-type clear cell) biology of the urodele pelvic gland; Kidney column shows full kidney identity (proximal SLC34A1/PAX8 3.3/2.9 + distal ATP6V1B1 2.07); prostate markers are too weak in this assembly (KLK1/KLK7 silent) to support any alternative.\n6. Residual uncertainty\n   - Some gastric markers are unreliable in this dataset: ATP4B, TFF2 (noisy paralog annotation), PGA3 (flat); PGC/MUC5AC paralog complexity; TFF3.2 high in the Cloaca column (possible cluster co-regulation with TFF2; axolotl TFF annotation is ambiguous).\n   - GallBladder sample is weak (lowest marker self-consistency; low correlation with everything) — identity cannot be independently verified, but no evidence it is swapped.\n   - UPK-like signal in Gill as above.\n7. Reproducibility: how to run analysis.py; outputs.\n\nConclusion: swap_detected=true, Cloaca↔Stomach, confidence 0.9.\n\nAlso mention the separation constraint: no public data was used (input only).\n\nLet me write it."}}
